e` and `highlighted`)\n  - NOT modify inline `<code>` (not inside `<pre>`)\n\n#### 4. taxonomy plugin\n- Factory: `createTaxonomyPlugin()`\n- Hooks: `beforeBuild` (build tag index), `afterBuild` (generate tag pages)\n- Must:\n  - Build tag index in `ctx.data` as `'taxonomy-tags'` (Map<string, ContentFile[]>)\n  - Generate tag pages in `ctx.output` at `tags/{tagname}.html`\n  - Handle files with no tags (empty map)\n  - Handle files with multiple tags\n  - Tag pages list all files with that tag\n  - Handle empty file list\n  - Case-normalize tags to lowercase\n\n### Wave 2 Plugins\n\n#### 5. rss-feed plugin\n- Factory: `createRssFeedPlugin()`\n- Dependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'taxonomy']`\n- Hook: `afterBuild`\n- Must:\n  - Generate `feed.xml` in `ctx.output`\n  - RSS 2.0 XML format\n  - Items contain CDATA-wrapped HTML content\n  - Include `<category>` tags from taxonomy\n  - Channel title from config\n  - Items sorted by date newest first\n  - Handle files without dates\n  - Link uses baseUrl\n\n#### 6. search-index plugin\n- Factory: `createSearchIndexPlugin()`\n- Dependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'syntax-highlight']`\n- Hook: `afterBuild`\n- Must:\n  - Generate `search-index.json` in `ctx.output`\n  - Entries have slug, title, content (plain text, HTML stripped)\n  - Code blocks included in text\n  - Handle empty file list\n  - Valid JSON\n  - Include tags if present\n\n### Wave 3 Plugins\n\n#### 7. sitemap plugin\n- Factory: `createSitemapPlugin()`\n- Dependencies: `['taxonomy', 'rss-feed']`\n- Hook: `afterBuild`\n- Must:\n  - Generate `sitemap.xml` in `ctx.output`\n  - Include all content page URLs (from `ctx.output`)\n  - Include taxonomy tag page URLs (from `ctx.data`)\n  - Include `feed.xml` URL (from `ctx.output`)\n  - URLs use baseUrl\n  - Valid XML structure\n  - Handle no tag pages\n\n#### 8. dev-server plugin\n- Factory: `createDevServerPlugin()`\n- Dependencies: `['asset-pipeline', 'search-index']`\n- Hook: `afterBuild`\n- Must:\n  - Store `'dev-server-config'` in `ctx.data`\n  - Config has `port` (number, default 3000)\n  - Include asset routes from `asset-manifest` data\n  - Include searchIndexPath (detects `search-index.json` in `ctx.output`)\n  - Include content routes (static) for HTML files in output\n  - Handle missing asset-pipeline data gracefully\n\nNow let me implement all plugins. I'll work test-first for each one.\n\nLet me start implementing. The tests are already written, so I just need to write the plugin implementations that pass them.\n\nLet me think about the `marked` library usage. The marked library is at ^17.0.2. The tests import from `../../src/plugins/markdown-ext.js` with `.js` extension (since it's ESM, Node16 module resolution requires extensions in imports).\n\nFor marked 17, the API uses `marked.parse()` which returns a string by default.\n\nFor the footnote extension, I need to handle `[^1]` syntax. Marked doesn't support footnotes natively. I'll need to use a `marked` extension to handle footnotes.\n\nActually, looking at marked plugins, I can use `marked-footnote` or write a custom extension. Let me check what's available. But the package.json only has `marked` as a dependency, not `marked-footnote`. So I'll need to handle footnotes manually (likely with regex), or I could write a custom marked extension.\n\nActually, looking at the marked docs, I can use `marked.use()` to add extensions. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nFor footnote syntax like:\n```\nSome text[^1] here.\n\n[^1]: This is the footnote.\n```\n\nI could pre-process the markdown to handle footnotes and admonitions before passing to marked, or I could post-process the HTML output.\n\nLet me think about the approach:\n1. Handle `:::note`, `:::warning`, `:::tip` containers — pre-process markdown to convert these into HTML divs\n2. Handle `[^1]` footnotes — post-process the HTML output\n\nActually, for admonitions, I could register a marked extension. Let me think about the cleanest approach.\n\nFor marked 17, extensions are registered with `marked.use()`. \n\nActually, the simplest approach might be:\n1. Pre-process the content to convert `:::note` / `:::warning` / `:::tip` blocks to HTML divs before passing to marked\n2. Pre-process footnotes into a format marked understands, or post-process\n\nWait, let me re-read the test expectations more carefully:\n\nTest 2: `content: 'Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n[^1]: This is the footnote.'`\nExpected: `<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>`\n\nTest 3: `content: 'Text[^1] and more[^2].\\n\\n[^1]: First note.\\n[^2]: Second note.'`\nExpected: `<section class=\"footnotes\">` and `<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>` and `<li id=\"fn-2\">Second note.</li>`\n\nSo the expected output has:\n- Footnote references: `<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>`\n- Footnote section: `<section class=\"footnotes\">` containing `<li id=\"fn-1\">`\n\nFor admonitions:\n- `:::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::` → `<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">\\nThis is a note.\\n</div>`\n\nLet me think about the simplest approach. I could:\n\n1. Convert `:::` blocks to `<div class=\"admonition admonition-xxx\">...` before passing to marked\n2. Handle footnotes with a custom marked extension or pre/post-process\n\nFor footnotes, I think the cleanest approach with marked is to write a custom extension. Let me look at how marked extensions work:\n\n```js\nconst footnoteExtension = {\n  name: 'footnote',\n  level: 'inline', // or 'block'\n  start(src) { return src.indexOf('[^'); },\n  tokenizer(src) { ... },\n  renderer(token) { ... },\n};\n```\n\nActually, for footnotes, the reference `[^1]` is inline and the definition `[^1]: text` is block-level. This is a complex extension. Let me think of a simpler approach.\n\nSince I need to handle both inline references and block definitions, I could:\n\n1. Pre-process: extract footnote definitions, replace inline `[^n]` with `<sup><a href=\"#fn-n\">n</a></sup>`\n2. Add footnote section at the end of the markdown/html content\n\nOr simpler: convert footnotes to HTML before passing to marked (which will ignore what looks like HTML in markdown).\n\nLet me write the code step by step.\n\nFor the markdown-ext plugin, I'll:\n1. Pre-process `:::` admonition blocks\n2. Pre-process `[^n]` footnotes (both references and definitions)\n3. Pass through marked for standard markdown\n4. Append footnote section\n\nActually let me try yet another approach. I'll handle:\n1. Pre-process: convert `:::` blocks to HTML\n2. Pre-process: extract footnote definitions from the markdown\n3. Pass through marked\n4. Post-process: replace `[^n]` with footnote superscript links in the HTML\n5. Append footnote section\n\nWait, but if I pre-process footnotes before marked, `[^1]` might be treated differently by marked. Let me test...\n\nActually, headed is probably the easiest approach:\n1. Pre-process `:::` blocks before marked\n2. For footnotes - I can use a custom marked extension, or handle it with regex.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The whole point is to augment marked to support:\n- Footnotes\n- Admonition containers\n\nI'll write a custom marked extension for footnotes, and pre-process admonitions.\n\nActually, the simplest and most maintainable approach:\n\n1. Handle `:::` blocks with regex (pre-process to HTML divs)\n2. Handle footnotes by:\n   a. Extract footnote definitions (`[^n]: text`) \n   b. Replace inline `[^n]` with superscript HTML\n   c. Run through marked\n   d. Append footnote section\n\nLet me write clean code for this.\n\nFor marked 17, the `marked` package exports a `parse` function. Let me check if `marked.parse()` or `marked()` is the right API. In marked 17, `marked.parse()` is the recommended API.\n\nLet me now implement all plugins. I'll start with Wave 1.\n\nFor the marked-footnotes, let me just write a regex-based approach since it's simpler than writing a full marked extension:\n\n```typescript\n// Pre-process markdown content\nfunction processMarkdown(content: string): string {\n  if (!content) return '';\n  \n  // Handle admonition containers :::note, :::warning, :::tip\n  content = content.replace(/:::(\\w+)\\n([\\s\\S]*?):::/g, \n    (_, type, body) => `<div class=\"admonition admonition-${type}\">\\n${body.trim()}\\n</div>`);\n  \n  // Extract footnote definitions\n  const footnotes: Array<{ id: string; text: string }> = [];\n  const footnoteRegex = /^\\[(\\^\\d+)\\]:\\s*(.+)$/gm;\n  let match;\n  while ((match = footnoteRegex.exec(content)) !== null) {\n    footnotes.push({ id: match[1], text: match[2] });\n  }\n  // Remove footnote definitions from content\n  content = content.replace(footnoteRegex, '');\n  \n  // Replace footnote references with superscript links\n  content = content.replace(/\\[\\^(\\d+)\\]/g, (_, num) => {\n    return `<sup><a href=\"#fn-${num}\">${num}</a></sup>`;\n  });\n  \n  // Parse with marked\n  let html = marked.parse(content);\n  \n  // Append footnote section\n  if (footnotes.length > 0) {\n    html += '\\n<section class=\"footnotes\">\\n<ol>\\n';\n    for (const fn of footnotes) {\n      const num = fn.id.replace('^', '');\n      html += `<li id=\"fn-${num}\">${fn.text}</li>\\n`;\n    }\n    html += '</ol>\\n</section>';\n  }\n  \n  return html;\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about the interleaving. Let me trace through test 2:\n\nInput: `'Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n[^1]: This is the footnote.'`\n\n1. Extract footnote definitions: `[^1]: This is the footnote.`\n   - footnotes = [{id: \"^1\", text: \"This is the footnote.\"}]\n   - Content becomes: `'Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n'`\n\n2. Replace `[^1]` with superscript link\n   - Content becomes: `'Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.\\n\\n'`\n\n3. Parse with marked: `<p>Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.</p>`\n\n4. Append footnotes: `<section class=\"footnotes\"><ol><li id=\"fn-1\">This is the footnote.</li></ol></section>`\n\n5. Final HTML: `<p>Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.</p>\\n<section class=\"footnotes\">\\n<ol>\\n<li id=\"fn-1\">This is the footnote.</li>\\n</ol>\\n</section>`\n\nAnd test 3 checks:\n- `result.content).toContain('<section class=\"footnotes\">')`\n- `result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>')`\n- `result.content).toContain('<li id=\"fn-2\">Second note.</li>')`\n\nSo the footnote section with `<li>` elements works. Good.\n\nBut wait - test 3 says `'<li id=\"fn-1\">First note.</li>'` which is the exact expected output. My approach would produce that.\n\nFor test 1:\n```\n# Hello\n\nThis is a **bold** paragraph.\n```\n→ marked.parse gives `<h1>Hello</h1>\\n<p>This is <strong>bold</strong> paragraph.</p>\\n`\n\nTest expects: `<h1>`, `Hello`, `<strong>bold</strong>`. ✓\n\nFor test 7 (existing HTML preserved):\n```\n<div class=\"custom\">Already HTML</div>\n```\n→ marked.parse with this gives `<div class=\"custom\">\\nAlready HTML\\n</div>\\n`\n\nWait, actually I need to check - does marked preserve raw HTML blocks by default? Yes, marked preserves inline HTML.\n\nBut actually marked might wrap it in `<p>` tags... Let me think. In markdown, a `<div>` on its own line is treated as a block-level HTML element and passed through verbatim.\n\nWith `marked.parse('<div class=\"custom\">Already HTML</div>')` in marked 17, I believe it would give something like:\n```\n<div class=\"custom\">\nAlready HTML\n</div>\n```\n\nThe test just checks `toContain('Already HTML')` so that's fine.\n\nNow for admonitions:\nTest 4: `':::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::'`\nExpected: `<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">` and `This is a note.`\n\nMy regex `:::(\\w+)\\n([\\s\\S]*?):::` would match:\n- type = \"note\"\n- body = \"This is a note.\\n\"\n\nWait, the `[\\s\\S]*?` is lazy, so it would match minimally. Let me think...\n\nInput: `:::note\\nThis is a note.\\n:::`\nThe regex: `/:::(\\w+)\\n([\\s\\S]*?):::/g`\n\nMatch 1: \n- `:::` matches literal\n- `(\\w+)` captures \"note\"\n- `\\n` matches newline\n- `([\\s\\S]*?)` lazily captures until first `:::`\n- `:::` matches literal\n\nSo body would be \"This is a note.\\n\" and replacement: `<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">\\nThis is a note.\\n\\n</div>`\n\nWait, body.trim() gives \"This is a note.\" and the replacement is:\n```\n<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">\nThis is a note.\n</div>\n```\n\nThen marked.parse would convert this (since it's HTML, it's passed through). Tests expect toContain the div and the text. ✓\n\nOK, let me also handle edge cases. For multiple admonitions or mixed content, the regex should work.\n\nNow, one issue: I'm processing `:::` blocks AND footnotes in the same pre-processing step. The order matters. I should process admonitions first (they're block-level), then footnotes (they're mixed inline/block).\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety. The footnote definition regex `\\[(\\^\\d+)\\]:\\s*(.+)$` uses `^` at the start of a line. After admonition processing, the content might have different line structure. But since admonitions are replaced with HTML blocks, the line-based footnote extraction should still work.\n\nActually let me reconsider. The footnote definition regex uses `^` which in multiline mode matches line starts. With the `m` flag... Actually in my regex I don't have the `m` flag. Let me use `m` flag with the regex.\n\nWait, `/^\\[(\\^\\d+)\\]:\\s*(.+)$/gm` - yes, `m` flag for multiline mode so `^` and `$` match line boundaries.\n\nLet me think about the content more carefully. After admonition processing:\n\n```\nSome text[^1] here.\n\n[^1]: This is the footnote.\n```\n\nAfter extracting footnotes:\n- Content becomes: \"Some text[^1] here.\\n\\n\"\n- Footnotes: [{id: \"^1\", text: \"This is the footnote.\"}]\n\nAfter replacing `[^1]` references:\n- Content becomes: \"Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.\\n\\n\"\n\nAfter marked.parse: `<p>Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.</p>\\n`\n\nAfter appending footnotes: `<p>Some text<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup> here.</p>\\n\\n<section class=\"footnotes\">\\n<ol>\\n<li id=\"fn-1\">This is the footnote.</li>\\n</ol>\\n</section>`\n\nThat should work.\n\nNow let me think about the asset-pipeline plugin. This one is simpler.\n\nFor `./image.png` → `/assets/image.png`:\n- Regex: find `src=\"./...\"` and `href=\"./...\"` patterns\n- Replace `./` prefix with `/assets/`\n- Collect asset filenames\n\nLooking at the tests:\n1. `<img src=\"./image.png\">` → `src=\"/assets/image.png\"`\n2. `<a href=\"./doc.pdf\">` → `href=\"/assets/doc.pdf\"`\n3. `src=\"https://cdn.example.com/image.png\"` unchanged\n4. Manifest: `ctx.data.set('asset-manifest', { assets: [...] })`\n5. No assets → unchanged\n6. Multiple assets in one file\n7. Accumulate across files (must aggregate, not reset)\n8. Already-rewritten `/assets/...` not double-processed\n\nFor the regex, I need to match:\n- `src=\"./` relative paths (images)\n- `href=\"./` relative paths (links)\n\nI should be careful about the pattern. Let me use: `(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+)\"`\n\nThis captures:\n- `src=\"./image.png\"` → groups: `src`, `image.png`\n- `href=\"./doc.pdf\"` → groups: `href`, `doc.pdf`\n\nReplace with: `$1=\"/assets/$2\"`\n\nAnd collect the filename from `$2` for the manifest.\n\nFor already-rewritten URLs (`/assets/...`), the regex won't match because the path starts with `/assets/` not `./`. ✓\n\nFor absolute URLs like `https://...`, they start with `https://` not `./`, so won't match. ✓\n\nFor manifest accumulation: since the hook is `transformContent` (called once per file), I need to accumulate state across calls. I can use a closure variable.\n\n```typescript\nexport function createAssetPipelinePlugin(): Plugin {\n  const assets: string[] = [];\n  \n  return {\n    name: 'asset-pipeline',\n    version: '1.0.0',\n    dependencies: [],\n    hooks: {\n      transformContent(file: ContentFile, ctx: BuildContext): ContentFile {\n        const content = file.content;\n        // Process and collect assets\n        // ...\n        return { ...file, content: transformedContent };\n      },\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, but when do I store the manifest in ctx.data? Since `transformContent` is called per-file, I should store it on every call (or in afterBuild). Let me check test 4:\n\n```typescript\nit('generates asset manifest in ctx.data', async () => {\n    const file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"./photo.jpg\">' });\n    const ctx = makeCtx();\n    await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, ctx);\n    const manifest = ctx.data.get('asset-manifest') as { assets: string[] };\n    expect(manifest).toBeDefined();\n    expect(manifest.assets).toContain('photo.jpg');\n});\n```\n\nThe test expects the manifest to be populated immediately after a single transformContent call. So I should set it during transformContent.\n\nAnd test 7 checks accumulation across files:\n```typescript\nawait plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file1, ctx);\nawait plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file2, ctx);\nconst manifest = ctx.data.get('asset-manifest') as { assets: string[] };\nexpect(manifest.assets).toHaveLength(2);\n```\n\nSo the manifest should reflect all assets found so far. I'll accumulate in a closure variable and set ctx.data on each call.\n\nTest 8:\n```typescript\nconst file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"/assets/image.png\">' });\nconst result = await plugin.hooks.transformContent!(file, ctx);\nexpect(result.content).toBe('<img src=\"/assets/image.png\">');\nconst manifest = ctx.data.get('asset-manifest') as { assets: string[] } | undefined;\nexpect(!manifest || manifest.assets.length === 0).toBe(true);\n```\n\nWait, this is interesting. The manifest might be undefined (if no transformContent calls found assets before) or have 0 assets. Since the transformation doesn't modify `/assets/image.png` and doesn't add it to the manifest, the assets array should remain empty.\n\nBut if I'm accumulating from a previous test... No wait, each test creates a new plugin instance. So the closure starts fresh.\n\nLet me also think about the regex more carefully. For `src=\"./path\"`, I want to replace to `src=\"/assets/path\"`. For `href=\"./path\"`, I want `href=\"/assets/path\"`. \n\nBut the pattern `(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+)\"` would match ALL such patterns. What if there's a relative path that's NOT an asset? The tests only test with images and PDFs, so I'll match all `./` paths.\n\nActually, let me re-read test 1:\n```typescript\nconst file = makeFile({ content: '<img src=\"./image.png\" alt=\"test\">' });\nexpect(result.content).toContain('src=\"/assets/image.png\"');\nexpect(result.content).not.toContain('src=\"./image.png\"');\n```\n\nAnd test 2:\n```typescript\nconst file = makeFile({ content: '<a href=\"./doc.pdf\">Download</a>' });\nexpect(result.content).toContain('href=\"/assets/doc.pdf\"');\nexpect(result.content).not.toContain('href=\"./doc.pdf\"');\n```\n\nSo it rewrites `src` and `href` with `./` prefix. I'll use the regex: `(src|href)=\"\\.\\/([^\"]+)\"` and replace with `$1=\"/assets/$2\"`.\n\nNow for collecting filenames: I capture `$2` which is the filename (e.g., `image.png`, `doc.pdf`).\n\nFor matching, I should use `content.matchAll` or a loop with `exec`.\n\nLet me code this up cleanly.\n\nNow for syntax-highlight plugin:\n\nInput: `<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\nOutput: `<pre data-language=\"javascript\"><code class=\"language-javascript highlighted\">const x = 1;</code></pre>`\n\nKey observations:\n1. Add `highlighted` class to `<code>` elements that already have a `language-*` class\n2. Add `data-language=\"xxx\"` to the parent `<pre>` tag\n3. If `<code>` has no language class, leave unchanged\n4. Inline `<code>` (not inside `<pre>`) should not be modified\n\nSo I need to match `<pre><code class=\"language-xxx\">...</code></pre>` patterns.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is regex replacement:\n\nPattern: `<pre><code class=\"language-(\\w+)\">(.*?)</code></pre>`\n\nWait, but this uses a lazy quantifier and wouldn't handle nested content correctly. But for code blocks, the content shouldn't have `</code></pre>` in it, so this should be safe.\n\nLet me also think about:\n- Content with newlines in code blocks: Test 7 has `<pre><code class=\"language-js\"></code></pre>` (empty)\n- For multiline, I need the `s` flag (dotAll) or use `[\\s\\S]*?`\n\nLet me use: `<pre><code class=\"language-(\\w+)\">([\\s\\S]*?)</code></pre>`\n\nReplace with: `<pre data-language=\"$1\"><code class=\"language-$1 highlighted\">$2</code></pre>`\n\nBut I also need to check: what if there are other attributes on `<code>`? The test input only has `class=\"language-xxx\"`, no other attributes. I'll keep it simple and match the exact pattern.\n\nFor test 4 (no language):\n```\n<pre><code>plain code</code></pre>\n```\n→ no `class=\"language-xxx\"` → regex doesn't match → unchanged ✓\n\nFor test 9 (inline code):\n```\n<p>Use <code>console.log()</code> for debugging.</p>\n```\n→ `<code>` not inside `<pre>` → regex doesn't match → unchanged ✓\n\nFor multiple code blocks: the global flag `g` handles all matches.\n\nNow for taxonomy plugin:\n\nThis has both `beforeBuild` and `afterBuild` hooks.\n\n`beforeBuild`: Build tag index from files\n- Iterate over `ctx.files`\n- For each file with `tags`, normalize to lowercase\n- Build Map<string, ContentFile[]>\n- Store in `ctx.data` as `'taxonomy-tags'`\n\n`afterBuild`: Generate tag pages\n- Read tag map from `ctx.data`\n- For each tag, generate HTML page listing files\n- Store in `ctx.output` as `tags/{tagname}.html`\n\nFor tag pages, what HTML format? The tests check:\n- `expect(ctx.output.get('tags/javascript.html')).toContain('Post 1');`\n- `expect(tagPage).toContain('First Post');`\n- `expect(tagPage).toContain('Second Post');`\n\nSo each tag page is just HTML that contains the titles of files with that tag. I'll generate simple HTML listing pages.\n\nLet me use a list-based format similar to what templates.ts does.\n\nNow for Wave 2:\n\n### rss-feed plugin\n\nDependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'taxonomy']`\nHook: `afterBuild`\n\nGenerate RSS 2.0 XML feed in `ctx.output` as `'feed.xml'`.\n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<rss version=\"2.0\">\n  <channel>\n    <title>Site Title</title>\n    <link>https://example.com</link>\n    <description>Site Description</description>\n    <item>\n      <title>Post Title</title>\n      <link>https://example.com/post-slug.html</link>\n      <guid>https://example.com/post-slug.html</guid>\n      <pubDate>Date</pubDate>\n      <description><![CDATA[HTML Content]]></description>\n      <category>tag</category>\n    </item>\n  </channel>\n</rss>\n```\n\nKey details from tests:\n1. `feed.xml` contains: `<?xml version=\"1.0\"`, `<rss version=\"2.0\">`, `<channel>`, `<item>`, `</rss>` ✓\n2. Content in CDATA: `<![CDATA[<p>This is <strong>HTML</strong> content</p>]]>` ✓\n3. Category tags from taxonomy data: `<category>javascript</category>` ✓\n4. Channel title: `<title>Test Site</title>` ✓\n5. Items sorted by date newest first ✓\n6. No `<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>` for files without dates ✓\n7. Links use baseUrl: `<link>https://example.com</link>` and `<link>https://example.com/post-1.html</link>` ✓\n\nFor test 3, it uses `ctx.data` with key `'taxonomy-tags'` which is the tag map.\n\nFor pubDate, I should format the date. RSS uses RFC 822 dates. But the test just checks no \"undefined\" appears. So I can format it properly or just omit pubDate for files without dates.\n\nActually, test 6: `expect(feed).not.toContain('<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>')`. So if I include `<pubDate>` with an undefined value, it would show `<pubDate>undefined</pubDate>`. I should skip pubDate for files without dates.\n\n### search-index plugin\n\nDependencies: `['markdown-ext', 'syntax-highlight']`\nHook: `afterBuild`\n\nGenerate `search-index.json` in `ctx.output`.\n\nEach entry: `{ slug, title, content (plain text), tags? }`\n\nFor stripping HTML: use regex to remove tags, or a simple HTML tag stripper.\n\nTest 3: HTML tags strippe